  and to the isle of man where the irish  language would eventually evolve into  two different languages scottish gaelic  or garlic as they call it  and manx by the 7th century archaic  irish had evolved into old irish  of which there are longer written texts  in existence old irish was written until  the 10th century  by which time it had already developed  into an early stage of middle irish  writings during the middle irish period  show emerging dialectal variation in the  different areas where it was spoken  the late old irish period and the early  middle irish period were characterized  by viking raids and norse settlement in  ireland  which had some impact on the vocabulary  of irish but the grammar wasn't really  affected  some of the norse loanwords that entered  irish are in  meaning penny and marugu meaning market  and some vocabulary relating to ships  and navigation  in the late 12th century the norman  invasion of ireland took place  when anglo-normans conquered and settled  the land  the language of the new ruling class was  norman french which was used alongside  irish and english the language of many  of the commoners who moved from england  to ireland during that time  in large part they were eventually  assimilated and became  more irish than the irish themselves and  irish became the most widely spoken  language again  but norman french left its mark on irish  in some dialects more than others  loanwords from norman french include  carson  meaning  meaning church and many more that was  all happening during the period of  classical irish  from the 13th to 18th centuries  classical irish was a literary language  learned by poets and scholars  and it remained relatively unchanged  during this time period  but the spoken language known as early  modern irish kept  gradually evolving towards modern irish  irish remains the most widely spoken  language until around the year 1800 but  english control meant that the english  language was the prestige language  and the more prosperous irish began  adopting the english language in greater  numbers  then the great famine of 1846 to 1848  disproportionately impacted the poor who  were mainly irish speakers  around 1 million people died and around  1 million emigrated during the famine  the irish language became seriously  endangered and limited to certain areas  most of them isolated rural areas  another factor in the decline of irish  was the introduction of national schools  state-funded primary schools which  prohibited the use of irish and enforced  an english-only policy  until 1871. in the late 19th century a  revival was underway with an irish  language newspaper  literature and successful efforts to  make the irish language part of the  school curriculum  part of this process was the development  of a standard language to unite the  various dialects  which was completed in 1958 now  irish is a living language with an  existing community of native speakers  it's the first official language of the  republic of ireland  and it's widely studied as a heritage  language in schools  but it's still considered endangered as  a spoken native language  something that many irish people hope to  change so  what is irish like phonology broad and  slender consonants  one important feature of irish is that  most written consonants have  two different pronunciations broad and  slender  broad consonants have a velarized  pronunciation meaning that the back of  your tongue  raises towards the soft palate or velum  when you produce the consonant sound  slender consonants have a palatalized  pronunciation  meaning that the middle of the tongue is  raised towards the hard palate  in modern irish orthography the adjacent  vowels  indicate whether the consonant is broad  or slender  the broad vowels are a o and u  and the narrow vowels are i and e  for example meaning what and  in the first word this a is a broad  vowel so the c  is pronounced as a broad consonant the  hard k sound  a voiceless velar stop the d is also  broad  this symbol here indicates valorization  the k here doesn't need that symbol  because it already represents a velar  consonant  in the second word this slender e vowel  is added to indicate that the c  is pronounced as a slender consonant in  other words it's palatalized  another example meaning pac  and meaning sin in the first word we see  the broad vowel  a twice so the p and the c both  represent  broad consonants in the second word  again the e is a slender vowel  so the p is palatalized in other words  slender  note that when you see a slender and  broad consonant side by side like this  the one closest to the consonant  indicates slenderness or broadness but  often  isn't pronounced as a vowel itself  another example  meaning yellow and e meaning be  this vowel and consonant are broad and  this vowel and consonant are slender  again this u is not pronounced as a  vowel but just  indicates that the preceding consonant  is broad the basic rule is  which means slender with slender broad  with broad  this means that in the middle of a word  slender vowels must appear on both sides  of a slender consonant  and broad vowels must appear on both  sides of a broad consonant  for example here the r or double r  must have a broad vowel both before and  after even though the one  after it is not pronounced as a vowel  itself  also when consonants appear together in  a cluster they must all share the same  broad or slender quality  for example meaning question both the s  and t are slender due to the preceding  eye vowel  initial consonant mutations another  feature of irish phonology  is initial consonant mutations a change  in a consonant at the beginning of a  word in certain grammatical contexts  there are two types of initial consonant  mutation  lenitin or chevu and eclipses  or first lunition in most cases but not  all  this type of mutation causes a stop to  become a fricative  for example bam meaning woman becomes  velm after the definite article in the  accusative case  a vam linition is shown in writing by  placing the letter  h after the original consonant another  example  is after singular possessive adjectives  take this word  meaning mother my mother would be moahes  notice that the initial consonant is  pronounced more like  rather than a bilabial nasal m sound  this type of mutation also occurs after  certain prepositions  and in other contexts next eclipses  this type of mutation usually involves a  voiceless stop becoming voiced  or a voiced stop becoming a nasal like  clinician  eclipses happens in certain grammatical  environments such as after plural  possessive adjectives  so the word for children is porshti but  to say our children  it's e this is shown in writing by  adding the letter  representing the new sound in front of  the original one  and it's used after some combinations of  dative prepositions plus the singular  definite article  for example bird meaning table but  on the table is is a murde and also  after the preposition  e without the definite article for  example  meaning house but in a house is  it's also used with certain numbers  board  boat shaft mode seven boats  by the way it's worth noting here that  in standard irish for non-human nouns  the singular form is used when counting  though in dialects the plural forms are  used as well  so why do these mutations appear in  these different environments that seem  to have little to do with each other  well it's because in an earlier stage of  irish they appeared in clear phonetic  environments  lenition occurred between vowels both  within words and across word boundaries  and eclipses took place after a nasal  sound their purpose was to allow  speech to flow without pauses some of  these vowel sounds and nasal sounds were  part of grammatical endings that have  since  disappeared so now the mutations  themselves indicate or give hints about  that grammatical information  prepositions another interesting element  of irish is its use of prepositions like  egg age  they can be used on their own with  definite articles or with personal  pronominal endings  when used alone or with a definite  article they work much the same way as  they do in english  but you might recall that in the  previous section on initial consonant  mutation  we saw that many prepositions when used  with the singular definite article cause  eclipses like in the phrase meaning on  the table  or is a mod meaning on the boat  the most interesting part is that you  can add a suffix to the preposition  which acts like a personal pronoun  so in the sentence meaning  he gave the money to me means to me  means to and the nasal means me  to us would be and so on  along with the suffix which is added the  preposition itself  also changes a little in many cases as  you can see in the parts that are  highlighted in blue  these are the inflected forms of the  prepositions  this preposition egg meaning at can be  used to show possession  since there's no separate word meaning  to have in irish  for example this sentence means i have a  brother  word for word it's is brother at me  numbers the number system in irish is  fairly complex  first of all there are three types of  cardinal numbers numbers used to count  disjunctive numbers these are numbers  that are used in calculations phone  numbers telling the time  and other contexts when they're not  counting a noun that follows  a particle a normally comes before them  with an h  prefix before initial vowels non-human  conjunctive numbers  these are numbers used to count  non-human nouns most of these are the  same as the disjunctive numbers without  the preceding  a or a h but note that the numbers 1  2 and 4 are different note that numbers  2 to 6  cause the noun to undergo lenition in  the initial consonant  and for the number one it's optional if  this word n  is also here and number seven to ten  caused the noun to undergo eclipses  human conjunctive numbers as you can  probably guess these are numbers used to  count human nouns  these ones are significantly different  in form from the others  notably in these suffixes on many of the  numbers  let's take the number eight as an  example if you're simply counting on  your fingers it's  if you're counting horses it's couple  if you're counting children it's  octoposty  if you're counting books it's lower  if you are counting sisters it's  that sentence means i have eight sisters  counting to two a doll two dogs  two children that's 40.  and there are also ordinal numbers  numbers we use to indicate order  like first second etc most of these are  related to the cardinal numbers  but have different suffixes or other  differences in form  but this one is completely different  verbs there are two categories of  regular verbs in irish  first conjugation and second conjugation  as well as irregular verbs  irish conjugations involve a combination  of suffixes and personal pronouns  in the present tense conjugations we see  here the first person singular and first  person plural have synthetic endings  endings that tell us person and number  so a subject pronoun isn't necessary  the other forms have an analytic ending  one that tells us nothing about person  and number so the verb needs to be  followed by a pronoun  first conjugation verbs and second  conjugation verbs  simply take somewhat different endings  in the first conjugation the endings are  m for the first person singular emits  for the first person plural  and um for the others that's when the  verb base contains a broad vowel before  the final consonant  if a slender vowel appears there then  the endings are  m ahmad um in the second conjugation the  endings are  imits in with a broad stem and  image in with a slender stem  that's just in the present tense there  are a variety of different tenses  including the imperfect  in other words the habitual past the  preterit in other words the simple past  the future and the conditional which all  use a combination of synthetic endings  and analytic endings with pronouns  some sentences now that we've examined a  few key features of irish let's look at  a few sentences and see what we find  before we briefly saw this sentence  meaning he gave me the money  okay let's take a closer look  word for word it's prefix  money to me first off we can see that  the most basic word order of irish is  vso  verb subject object this word  is the past indicative analytic form of  the verb  always like we saw with the present  tense verb forms before  analytic means that this verb form  doesn't tell us anything about person or  number so a pronoun is needed  and this is the pronoun right here means  he  next is the singular definite article  this word is the word for money and like  we saw before  this word is an inflected preposition  a combination of meaning to and the  prefix  meaning me now what's this t doing here  this is similar to the initial consonant  mutations that we saw before  it's inserted between the article um and  nouns beginning with a vowel  it's also inserted before nouns  beginning in s in some contexts  for example swords meaning street  and meaning the street the specifics of  when this happens are somewhat  complicated as they are with the  mutations that we saw before  another sentence this one means i have  to get up early  word for word it's is on me rise  adverb particle early the first word  is the present indicative analytic  habitual form of the verb be  this present tense form applies to  general actions and states that are  habitual  not to those that are only true for the  current moment so this sentence means i  have to get up early  on a regular basis not just once to say  i have to get up early as in  only tomorrow it would be galore  this is also a present tense form of the  verb be  but is not habitual next we see another  inflected preposition  which means on me so saying it is on me  expresses obligation in irish next we  have a verb meaning  to rise this form is a verbal noun which  is often used where we would use the two  infinitive in english and where the  present participle the ing form is used  in english  next we see the word go which has a  number of different usages  but in this case it functions to turn  the next word into an adverb  loa is an adjective meaning fast or  early  but together with it functions as an  adverb  let's go back to the first word for a  minute like i said before  this is a form of the verb be which  means to be  this is a special verb with many  different forms that other verbs don't  have  including the present habitual that we  saw before and a special negative form  that negates the regular present tense  so we have to autumn meaning i need to  wake up early  and that becomes meaning i don't have to  wake up early  the highlighted words are all forms of  the verb be  but this is not the only verb meaning to  be in irish  there's another verb is is is used when  you want to say what someone or  something  is one noun or pronoun is equivalent to  another noun or pronoun  the verb be is used when you want to  describe someone or something  so there's often a noun or a pronoun  with an adjective to describe it  so we have it's moon thoughty meaning  she's a teacher  and meaning she is kind  but means she's a kind person  here kind person is a noun phrase so we  use is  also note the difference in the personal  pronouns e  is a form of the personal pronoun that  is used specifically with is  she is the regular subject form  wow there's a lot to take in when  dealing with the irish language  it sure is interesting but it's also a  little overwhelming and  intimidating every aspect of the  language seems to be filled with details  and rules that need to be learned and  practiced  but in spite of that or maybe because of  that a lot of people who choose to study  irish for their own reasons really go  deep down the rabbit hole and get into  it and study it passionately  in this video we can only barely scratch  the surface but if you decide to study  the irish language  a fascinating journey awaits you the  question of the day  for learners of irish what's been your  experience in learning the language what  have you found challenging what have you  found interesting  and for native speakers of irish what  elements of the language do you think  that learners need to focus on the most  a number of native speakers have told me  how disturbed they are by the lack of  authentic pronunciation among learners  of irish  they were referring specifically to  people who learn irish at school  they told me that most teachers of irish  don't know authentic  irish phonology so they teach irish with  english phonemes  missing the distinction between broad  and slender consonants for example  do you agree with that assessment be  sure to follow langfocus on facebook  twitter and  instagram and as always many many thanks  to my patreon supporters especially  my top tier patreon supporters these  lovely people right here on the screen  many many thanks to them and to everyone  thank you for watching and have a nice  day  [Music]  so  you

