 and the conditional which all  use a combination of synthetic endings  and analytic endings with pronouns  some sentences now that we've examined a  few key features of irish let's look at  a few sentences and see what we find  before we briefly saw this sentence  meaning he gave me the money  okay let's take a closer look  word for word it's prefix  money to me first off we can see that  the most basic word order of irish is  vso  verb subject object this word  is the past indicative analytic form of  the verb  always like we saw with the present  tense verb forms before  analytic means that this verb form  doesn't tell us anything about person or  number so a pronoun is needed  and this is the pronoun right here means  he  next is the singular definite article  this word is the word for money and like  we saw before  this word is an inflected preposition  a combination of meaning to and the  prefix  meaning me now what's this t doing here  this is similar to the initial consonant  mutations that we saw before  it's inserted between the article um and  nouns beginning with a vowel  it's also inserted before nouns  beginning in s in some contexts  for example swords meaning street  and meaning the street the specifics of  when this happens are somewhat  complicated as they are with the  mutations that we saw before  another sentence this one means i have  to get up early  word for word it's is on me rise  adverb particle early the first word  is the present indicative analytic  habitual form of the verb be  this present tense form applies to  general actions and states that are  habitual  not to those that are only true for the  current moment so this sentence means i  have to get up early  on a regular basis not just once to say  i have to get up early as in  only tomorrow it would be galore  this is also a present tense form of the  verb be  but is not habitual next we see another  inflected preposition  which means on me so saying it is on me  expresses obligation in irish next we  have a verb meaning  to rise this form is a verbal noun which  is often used where we would use the two  infinitive in english and where the  present participle the ing form is used  in english  next we see the word go which has a  number of different usages  but in this case it functions to turn  the next word into an adverb  loa is an adjective meaning fast or  early  but together with it functions as an  adverb  let's go back to the first word for a  minute like i said before  this is a form of the verb be which  means to be  this is a special verb with many  different forms that other verbs don't  have  including the present habitual that we  saw before and a special negative form  that negates the regular present tense  so we have to autumn meaning i need to  wake up early  and that becomes meaning i don't have to  wake up early  the highlighted words are all forms of  the verb be  but this is not the only verb meaning to  be in irish  there's another verb is is is used when  you want to say what someone or  something  is one noun or pronoun is equivalent to  another noun or pronoun  the verb be is used when you want to  describe someone or something  so there's often a noun or a pronoun  with an adjective to describe it  so we have it's moon thoughty meaning  she's a teacher  and meaning she is kind  but means she's a kind person  here kind person is a noun phrase so we  use is  also note the difference in the personal  pronouns e  is a form of the personal pronoun that  is used specifically with is  she is the regular subject form  wow there's a lot to take in when  dealing with the irish language  it sure is interesting but it's also a  little overwhelming and  intimidating every aspect of the  language seems to be filled with details  and rules that need to be learned and  practiced  but in spite of that or maybe because of  that a lot of people who choose to study  irish for their own reasons really go  deep down the rabbit hole and get into  it and study it passionately  in this video we can only barely scratch  the surface but if you decide to study  the irish language  a fascinating journey awaits you the  question of the day  for learners of irish what's been your  experience in learning the language what  have you found challenging what have you  found interesting  and for native speakers of irish what  elements of the language do you think  that learners need to focus on the most  a number of native speakers have told me  how disturbed they are by the lack of  authentic pronunciation among learners  of irish  they were referring specifically to  people who learn irish at school  they told me that most teachers of irish  don't know authentic  irish phonology so they teach irish with  english phonemes  missing the distinction between broad  and slender consonants for example  do you agree with that assessment be  sure to follow langfocus on facebook  twitter and  instagram and as always many many thanks  to my patreon supporters especially  my top tier patreon supporters these  lovely people right here on the screen  many many thanks to them and to everyone  thank you for watching and have a nice  day  [Music]  so  you
 

